  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "q:u1-1-1:001",
      "lo": "lo:u1-1-1",
      "tier": "basic",
      "type": "mcq",
      "stem": "If $(a, 5) = (3, b)$, then $a + b =$ ?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "8"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "15"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "2"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "35"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "A",
      "solution": [
        "Two ordered pairs are equal when their corresponding projections are equal: $(a,b)=(x,y) \\Rightarrow a=x,\\ b=y$.",
        "So $a = 3$ and $b = 5$.",
        "Therefore $a + b = 3 + 5 = 8$."
      ],
      "source_page": 7,
      "source_note": "Original question; scope of Lesson 1-1, equality of ordered pairs (cf. Example 1, p.7)"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:u1-1-1:002",
      "lo": "lo:u1-1-1",
      "tier": "standard",
      "type": "numeric",
      "stem": "If $(x - 4,\\ 7) = (9,\\ y + 2)$, find the value of $x \\times y$.",
      "answer": "65",
      "solution": [
        "Equate first projections: $x - 4 = 9$, so $x = 13$.",
        "Equate second projections: $7 = y + 2$, so $y = 5$.",
        "Therefore $x \\times y = 13 \\times 5 = 65$."
      ],
      "source_page": 7,
      "source_note": "Original question; scope of Lesson 1-1 drill, p.7"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:u1-1-1:003",
      "lo": "lo:u1-1-1",
      "tier": "advanced",
      "type": "mcq",
      "stem": "If $(a - 2,\\ b + 1) = (b - 4,\\ 3)$, then the ordered pair $(a, b) =$ ?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "$(0, 2)$"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "$(4, 2)$"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "$(2, 4)$"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "$(-2, 2)$"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "A",
      "solution": [
        "From the second projections: $b + 1 = 3$, so $b = 2$.",
        "From the first projections: $a - 2 = b - 4 = 2 - 4 = -2$, so $a = 0$.",
        "Therefore $(a, b) = (0, 2)$."
      ],
      "source_page": 7,
      "source_note": "Original question; extends Lesson 1-1 equality of ordered pairs"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:u1-1-2:001",
      "lo": "lo:u1-1-2",
      "tier": "basic",
      "type": "mcq",
      "stem": "If $X = \\{1, 2\\}$ and $Y = \\{5\\}$, then $X \\times Y =$ ?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "$\\{(1,5), (2,5)\\}$"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "$\\{(5,1), (5,2)\\}$"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "$\\{(1,2), (5,5)\\}$"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "$\\{1, 2, 5\\}$"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "A",
      "solution": [
        "$X \\times Y$ is the set of ordered pairs whose first projection is in $X$ and second projection is in $Y$.",
        "Pair each element of $X$ with each element of $Y$: $(1,5)$ and $(2,5)$.",
        "So $X \\times Y = \\{(1,5), (2,5)\\}$."
      ],
      "source_page": 8,
      "source_note": "Original question; scope of Lesson 1-1, Example 2, p.8"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:u1-1-2:002",
      "lo": "lo:u1-1-2",
      "tier": "standard",
      "type": "numeric",
      "stem": "If $n(X) = 3$ and $n(Y) = 4$, find $n(X \\times Y)$.",
      "answer": "12",
      "solution": [
        "For finite non-empty sets, $n(X \\times Y) = n(X) \\times n(Y)$.",
        "$n(X \\times Y) = 3 \\times 4 = 12$."
      ],
      "source_page": 8,
      "source_note": "Original question; property 2, p.8"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:u1-1-2:003",
      "lo": "lo:u1-1-2",
      "tier": "advanced",
      "type": "numeric",
      "stem": "If $n(X \\times Y) = 15$ and $n(X) = 3$, find $n(Y^2)$.",
      "answer": "25",
      "solution": [
        "$n(X \\times Y) = n(X) \\times n(Y)$, so $15 = 3 \\times n(Y)$, giving $n(Y) = 5$.",
        "$Y^2 = Y \\times Y$, so $n(Y^2) = n(Y) \\times n(Y) = 5 \\times 5 = 25$."
      ],
      "source_page": 8,
      "source_note": "Original question; combines n(X×Y) property with X² notation, p.8"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:u1-1-3:001",
      "lo": "lo:u1-1-3",
      "tier": "basic",
      "type": "mcq",
      "stem": "In the arrow diagram of $X \\times Y$, each arrow starts from…",
      "choices": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "an element of $X$ (the first projection)"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "an element of $Y$ (the second projection)"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "the origin point $O$"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "any element of $X$ or $Y$"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "A",
      "solution": [
        "The arrow diagram represents each ordered pair by an arrow from its first projection to its second projection.",
        "First projections are the elements of $X$, so every arrow starts from an element of $X$."
      ],
      "source_page": 10,
      "source_note": "Original question; arrow diagram definition, p.10"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:u1-1-3:002",
      "lo": "lo:u1-1-3",
      "tier": "standard",
      "type": "numeric",
      "stem": "If $X = \\{a, b\\}$ and $Y = \\{1, 2, 3\\}$, how many arrows are in the arrow diagram of $X \\times Y$?",
      "answer": "6",
      "solution": [
        "Each ordered pair in $X \\times Y$ is represented by one arrow.",
        "$n(X \\times Y) = n(X) \\times n(Y) = 2 \\times 3 = 6$.",
        "So the diagram has 6 arrows."
      ],
      "source_page": 10,
      "source_note": "Original question; arrow diagram + counting, p.10"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:u1-1-4:001",
      "lo": "lo:u1-1-4",
      "tier": "basic",
      "type": "mcq",
      "stem": "The point $(-2, 5)$ is located in the…",
      "choices": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "second quadrant"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "first quadrant"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "third quadrant"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "fourth quadrant"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "A",
      "solution": [
        "In the second quadrant, $x < 0$ and $y > 0$.",
        "Here $x = -2 < 0$ and $y = 5 > 0$, so the point lies in the second quadrant."
      ],
      "source_page": 12,
      "source_note": "Original question; quadrants of R×R, p.12 (cf. Example 6)"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:u1-1-4:002",
      "lo": "lo:u1-1-4",
      "tier": "standard",
      "type": "mcq",
      "stem": "The point $(0, -3)$ is located…",
      "choices": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "on the y-axis"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "on the x-axis"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "in the third quadrant"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "in the fourth quadrant"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "A",
      "solution": [
        "A point with first coordinate $0$ lies on the y-axis.",
        "$(0, -3)$ has $x = 0$, so it lies on the y-axis (below the origin)."
      ],
      "source_page": 12,
      "source_note": "Original question; points on axes, Example 6, p.12"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:u1-2-1:001",
      "lo": "lo:u1-2-1",
      "tier": "basic",
      "type": "mcq",
      "stem": "If $R$ is a relation from $X$ to $Y$, then $R$ is a subset of…",
      "choices": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "$X \\times Y$"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "$Y \\times X$"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "$X$"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "$Y$"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "A",
      "solution": [
        "A relation from $X$ to $Y$ connects elements of $X$ with elements of $Y$ using ordered pairs $(a, b)$ with $a \\in X$, $b \\in Y$.",
        "All such pairs belong to $X \\times Y$, so $R \\subseteq X \\times Y$."
      ],
      "source_page": 14,
      "source_note": "Original question; deduction 3, p.14"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:u1-2-1:002",
      "lo": "lo:u1-2-1",
      "tier": "standard",
      "type": "mcq",
      "stem": "If $X = \\{1, 2\\}$, $Y = \\{2, 4, 6\\}$ and $R$ is a relation from $X$ to $Y$ where $a\\,R\\,b$ means $b = 2a$, then $R =$ ?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "$\\{(1,2), (2,4)\\}$"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "$\\{(2,1), (4,2)\\}$"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "$\\{(1,2), (2,4), (3,6)\\}$"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "$\\{(1,4), (2,2)\\}$"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "A",
      "solution": [
        "Test each $a \\in X$: for $a = 1$, $b = 2(1) = 2 \\in Y$ ✓; for $a = 2$, $b = 2(2) = 4 \\in Y$ ✓.",
        "So $R = \\{(1,2), (2,4)\\}$. Note $(3,6)$ is impossible since $3 \\notin X$."
      ],
      "source_page": 13,
      "source_note": "Original question; scope of Example 1, p.13"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:u1-2-1:003",
      "lo": "lo:u1-2-1",
      "tier": "advanced",
      "type": "numeric",
      "stem": "If $n(X) = 2$ and $n(Y) = 3$, how many different relations can be defined from $X$ to $Y$? (Hint: every subset of $X \\times Y$ is a relation.)",
      "answer": "64",
      "solution": [
        "$n(X \\times Y) = 2 \\times 3 = 6$.",
        "Every subset of $X \\times Y$ is a relation from $X$ to $Y$, and a set with 6 elements has $2^6$ subsets.",
        "$2^6 = 64$ relations."
      ],
      "source_page": 14,
      "source_note": "Original stretch question; builds on R ⊆ X×Y, p.14"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:u1-2-2:001",
      "lo": "lo:u1-2-2",
      "tier": "standard",
      "type": "mcq",
      "stem": "If $X = \\{-1, 0, 1\\}$ and $R$ is a relation on $X$ where $a\\,R\\,b$ means $a + b = 0$, then $R =$ ?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "$\\{(-1,1), (0,0), (1,-1)\\}$"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "$\\{(-1,1), (1,-1)\\}$"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "$\\{(0,0)\\}$"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "$\\{(-1,-1), (1,1)\\}$"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "A",
      "solution": [
        "A relation on $X$ pairs elements of $X$ with elements of $X$.",
        "Check all pairs with $a + b = 0$: $(-1, 1)$, $(0, 0)$, $(1, -1)$.",
        "So $R = \\{(-1,1), (0,0), (1,-1)\\}$ — don't forget $(0,0)$, since $0 + 0 = 0$."
      ],
      "source_page": 14,
      "source_note": "Original question; scope of Example 2 (additive inverse relation), p.14"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:u1-3-1:001",
      "lo": "lo:u1-3-1",
      "tier": "basic",
      "type": "mcq",
      "stem": "A relation from $X$ to $Y$ is a function if…",
      "choices": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "each element of $X$ appears exactly once as a first projection"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "each element of $Y$ appears exactly once as a second projection"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "it contains all the elements of $X \\times Y$"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "the number of its pairs equals $n(X \\times Y)$"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "A",
      "solution": [
        "By definition (Lesson 1-3): a relation from $X$ to $Y$ is a function if each element of $X$ appears only once as a first projection in the ordered pairs.",
        "Elements of $Y$ may repeat or be unused — the condition is only on $X$."
      ],
      "source_page": 16,
      "source_note": "Original question; function definition, p.16"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:u1-3-1:002",
      "lo": "lo:u1-3-1",
      "tier": "standard",
      "type": "mcq",
      "stem": "If $X = \\{1, 2, 3\\}$, which of the following relations on $X$ is a function on $X$?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "$\\{(1,2), (2,3), (3,1)\\}$"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "$\\{(1,2), (1,3), (2,1), (3,2)\\}$"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "$\\{(1,2), (2,3)\\}$"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "$\\{(2,2), (3,3)\\}$"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "A",
      "solution": [
        "A function on $X$ needs every element of $X$ to appear exactly once as a first projection.",
        "Option B: 1 appears twice as a first projection ✗. Options C and D: the element 3 (resp. 1) never appears as a first projection ✗.",
        "Option A: 1, 2, 3 each appear exactly once as first projections ✓."
      ],
      "source_page": 16,
      "source_note": "Original question; scope of Drill 1, p.17"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:u1-3-1:003",
      "lo": "lo:u1-3-1",
      "tier": "advanced",
      "type": "mcq",
      "stem": "$X = \\{2, 3, 4\\}$, $Y = \\{y : y \\in \\mathbb{N},\\ 2 \\le y < 9\\}$, and $R$ from $X$ to $Y$ where $a\\,R\\,b$ means $a = \\frac{1}{2}b$. Is $R$ a function from $X$ to $Y$, and what is $R$?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "Yes; $R = \\{(2,4), (3,6), (4,8)\\}$"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "No; $R = \\{(2,4), (3,6)\\}$"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "Yes; $R = \\{(4,2), (6,3), (8,4)\\}$"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "No; because 8 is not in $Y$"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "A",
      "solution": [
        "$Y = \\{2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8\\}$.",
        "$a = \\frac{1}{2}b$ means $b = 2a$: for $a=2, b=4$; $a=3, b=6$; $a=4, b=8$ — all in $Y$.",
        "Every element of $X$ appears exactly once as a first projection, so $R$ is a function: $R = \\{(2,4), (3,6), (4,8)\\}$."
      ],
      "source_page": 18,
      "source_note": "Original variant of Example 2, p.18 (same solution skeleton, same sets)"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:u1-3-2:001",
      "lo": "lo:u1-3-2",
      "tier": "standard",
      "type": "mcq",
      "stem": "If $f = \\{(-1, 3), (2, 5), (3, 7)\\}$ is a function from $X = \\{-1, 2, 3\\}$ to $Y = \\{2, 3, 5, 7\\}$, then the range of $f$ = ?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "$\\{3, 5, 7\\}$"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "$\\{2, 3, 5, 7\\}$"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "$\\{-1, 2, 3\\}$"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "$\\{2\\}$"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "A",
      "solution": [
        "The range is the set of images of the elements of the domain — the second projections actually used.",
        "Images: $f(-1)=3$, $f(2)=5$, $f(3)=7$.",
        "Range $= \\{3, 5, 7\\}$; note it is a subset of the codomain $Y$, and $2 \\in Y$ is not an image."
      ],
      "source_page": 17,
      "source_note": "Original question; domain/codomain/range example, p.17"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:u1-3-2:002",
      "lo": "lo:u1-3-2",
      "tier": "advanced",
      "type": "mcq",
      "stem": "For $f : X \\to Y$ where $X = \\{2, 3, 4\\}$ and $f(x) = 2x$, which statement is correct?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "The range is $\\{4, 6, 8\\}$ and it must be a subset of $Y$"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "The domain is $\\{4, 6, 8\\}$"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "The codomain must equal $\\{4, 6, 8\\}$"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "The range equals $X$"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "A",
      "solution": [
        "The domain is $X = \\{2,3,4\\}$; the images are $f(2)=4$, $f(3)=6$, $f(4)=8$.",
        "The range $\\{4,6,8\\}$ is the set of images, and the range is always a subset of the codomain $Y$.",
        "The codomain may be larger than the range, so C is wrong."
      ],
      "source_page": 17,
      "source_note": "Original question; range ⊆ codomain note, p.17"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:u1-4-1:001",
      "lo": "lo:u1-4-1",
      "tier": "basic",
      "type": "mcq",
      "stem": "Which of the following represents a polynomial function?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "$f(x) = x^3 + x^2 + 3$"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "$f(x) = x^3 + \\frac{1}{x} + 7$"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "$f(x) = x^2 + \\sqrt{x} + 8$"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "$f(x) = \\frac{2}{x^2}$"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "A",
      "solution": [
        "A polynomial has the form $f(x) = a_0 + a_1 x + a_2 x^2 + \\dots + a_n x^n$ with natural-number powers of $x$ only.",
        "$\\frac{1}{x} = x^{-1}$ and $\\sqrt{x} = x^{1/2}$ are not natural powers, so B, C, D are excluded.",
        "$f(x) = x^3 + x^2 + 3$ is a polynomial (degree 3)."
      ],
      "source_page": 19,
      "source_note": "Original question; scope of Drill 1, p.19"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:u1-4-1:002",
      "lo": "lo:u1-4-1",
      "tier": "standard",
      "type": "numeric",
      "stem": "If $f(x) = x^2 - x + 3$, find $f(-2)$.",
      "answer": "9",
      "solution": [
        "Substitute $x = -2$ into the rule of the function.",
        "$f(-2) = (-2)^2 - (-2) + 3 = 4 + 2 + 3$.",
        "$f(-2) = 9$."
      ],
      "source_page": 20,
      "source_note": "Original variant of Example 1, p.20 (same solution skeleton)"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:u1-4-1:003",
      "lo": "lo:u1-4-1",
      "tier": "advanced",
      "type": "numeric",
      "stem": "If $f(x) = x^2 - 3x$ and $g(x) = x - 3$, find the value of $f(3) + g(3)$.",
      "answer": "0",
      "solution": [
        "$f(3) = 3^2 - 3(3) = 9 - 9 = 0$.",
        "$g(3) = 3 - 3 = 0$.",
        "$f(3) + g(3) = 0 + 0 = 0$."
      ],
      "source_page": 20,
      "source_note": "Original variant of Drill B, p.20 (proves f(3) = g(3) = 0)"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:u1-4-1:004",
      "lo": "lo:u1-4-1",
      "tier": "standard",
      "type": "mcq",
      "stem": "The degree of the polynomial function $f(x) = x(x - 2x^2)$ is…",
      "choices": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "3"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "2"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "1"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "4"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "A",
      "solution": [
        "Expand: $f(x) = x \\cdot x - x \\cdot 2x^2 = x^2 - 2x^3$.",
        "The degree is the highest power of $x$, which is 3."
      ],
      "source_page": 19,
      "source_note": "Original question; scope of Drill 2C, p.19"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:u1-4-2:001",
      "lo": "lo:u1-4-2",
      "tier": "basic",
      "type": "mcq",
      "stem": "The graph of $f(x) = ax$ where $a \\neq 0$ is a straight line that passes through…",
      "choices": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "the origin $(0, 0)$"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "the point $(0, a)$"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "the point $(a, 0)$"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "no fixed point"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "A",
      "solution": [
        "$f(0) = a \\times 0 = 0$, so the point $(0, 0)$ always lies on the graph.",
        "The graph of $f(x) = ax$, $a \\neq 0$, is a straight line through the origin (Remark 2, Lesson 1-4)."
      ],
      "source_page": 21,
      "source_note": "Original question; Remark 2, p.21"
    },
    {
      "id": "q:u1-4-2:002",
      "lo": "lo:u1-4-2",
      "tier": "standard",
      "type": "mcq",
      "stem": "Which point lies on the graph of the linear function $f(x) = 2x - 3$?",
      "choices": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "$(2, 1)$"
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "$(1, 1)$"
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "$(0, 3)$"
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "$(3, 2)$"
        }
      ],
      "answer": "A",
      "solution": [
        "A point $(x, y)$ lies on the graph when $y = f(x)$.",
        "Test A: $f(2) = 2(2) - 3 = 1$ ✓, so $(2, 1)$ lies on the graph.",
        "(Check B: $f(1) = -1 \\neq 1$; C: $f(0) = -3 \\neq 3$; D: $f(3) = 3 \\neq 2$.)"
      ],
      "source_page": 20,
      "source_note": "Original question; scope of Example 2, p.20"
    },
    {
